INSERT INTO `books` VALUES (1,'To Kill a Mockingbird','Harper Lee','Fiction',3,1,'no',0,'classic, racism, justice','Set in the fictional town of Maycomb, Alabama, during the Great Depression, the story follows youngJean Louise \"Scout\" Finch, her brother Jem, and their friend Dill as they grow up under the guidance of Scout\'s widowed father, Atticus Finch. When Atticus, a respected local lawyer, is appointed to defend Tom Robinson—a Black man falsely accused of assaulting a white woman—the children witness firsthand the deep-seated prejudice and moral hypocrisy of their community.\n\nThrough Scout\'s innocent yet observant perspective, the novel weaves together the courtroom drama with the children\'s fascination with their reclusive neighbor, Boo Radley. It remains an enduring exploration of racial injustice, moral courage, empathy, and the loss of childhood innocence.'),(2,'A Brief History of Time','Stephen Hawking','Science',3,0,'no',0,'cosmology, universe, physics','Written for readers with no prior background in theoretical physics, Stephen Hawking guides audiences through the most profound questions about the nature of the cosmos. Beginning with historical perspectives from Aristotle, Galileo, and Newton, the book traces the evolution of human understanding up to Einstein\'s general theory of relativity and the birth of quantum mechanics.\n\nHawking examines the origins of the universe in the Big Bang, the puzzling mechanics of black holes, the nature of space and time, and the ongoing search for a unified theory of everything that would reconcile gravity with the quantum realm.'),(3,'Sapiens A Brief History of Humankind','Yuval Noah Harari','History',3,0,'no',0,'evolution, anthropology, civilization','Spanning 70,000 years of history, Yuval Noah Harari traces how an insignificant ape on the African savanna rose to become the undisputed ruler of planet Earth. The narrative is structured around three major turning points: the Cognitive Revolution, which gave humans the ability to create shared myths and cooperate in large numbers; the Agricultural Revolution, which domesticated both plants and humanity itself; and the Scientific Revolution, which unlocked unprecedented technological power.\n\nBy blending history, evolutionary biology, and economics, the book challenges conventional assumptions about progress, examining how empires, religion, capitalism, and science have shaped modern societies and where biotechnology might lead our species next.'),(4,'Clean Code','Robert C. Martin','Technology',3,0,'no',0,'programming, software, development','Even bad code can function, but if code isn\'t clean, it can bring a development organization to its knees through technical debt and endless maintenance bottlenecks. Robert C. Martin presents a comprehensive handbook of agile software craftsmanship, dividing the material into foundational principles, practical patterns, and hands-on case studies of increasing complexity.\n\nReaders learn how to choose meaningful names, write focused functions, format code for maximum readability, handle errors without obscuring logic, and implement effective unit tests. By walking through real-world refactoring exercises, the book trains developers to transform fragile, convoluted codebases into clean, maintainable systems.'),(5,'Mathematics Its Content Methods and Meaning','A.D. Aleksandrov','Mathematics',3,0,'no',0,'theory, algebra, geometry','Originally compiled by a distinguished team of Soviet mathematicians for a broad educated audience, this monumental work provides a sweeping survey of modern mathematics, its historical development, and its practical applications. Rather than treating branches of math in isolation, the authors emphasize how arithmetic, geometry, algebra, and analysis grew out of real-world problems and evolved into rigorous abstract systems.\n\nAcross its sections, the book unpacks analytic geometry, differential and integral calculus, differential equations, complex variables, prime numbers, probability, and topology, illuminating how mathematical thought underpins modern physics, engineering, and logic.'),(6,'Pride and Prejudice','Jane Austen','Literature',2,1,'yes',1,'romance, society, classic','When the wealthy and amiable Charles Bingley rents Netherfield Park, the news sends ripples of excitement through the neighboring Bennet family, particularly Mrs. Bennet, who is desperate to see her five daughterssecure advantageous marriages. Bingley\'s arrival also introduces his proud, reserved friend Fitzwilliam Darcy, whose initial arrogance immediately alienates the spirited and quick-witted Elizabeth Bennet.\n\nAs misunderstandings, social scheming, and family scandals unfold across Regency-era England, both Elizabeth and Darcy are forced to confront their own blind spots. Their turbulent courtship becomes a sharp, witty examination of social class, reputation, marriage, and the transformative power of self-awareness.'),(7,'The Story of Art','E.H. Gombrich','Art',2,0,'no',0,'history, painting, sculpture','First published in 1950 and revered as one of the most accessible introductions to visual culture ever written, E.H. Gombrich\'s survey approaches art history not as a dry catalog of dates and styles, but as a continuous, living chain of human creativity. Opening with the famous declaration that \"there really is no such thing as Art—there are only artists,\" the narrative traces how each generation builds upon or rebels against the visual solutions of its predecessors.\n\nMoving chronologically from prehistoric cave paintings and Egyptian monuments through the Renaissance masters to modern experimental movements, Gombrich places every masterpiece in its social, religious, and technical context, making complex artistic shifts effortless to grasp.'),(8,'The Republic','Plato','Philosophy',2,0,'no',0,'ethics, politics, justice','Structured as a series of lively Socratic dialogues, Plato\'s masterwork begins with a seemingly simple question: What is justice, and why should a person choose to be just rather than unjust? To answer this, Socrates proposes constructing an ideal city-state in speech, reasoning that justice written on the large scale of a society will make it easier to identify within the individual human soul.\n\nAlong the way, the dialogue explores the division of labor, the education of the \"Guardians,\" the rule of philosopher-kings, the immortality of the soul, and the famous Allegory of the Cave. Its arguments have served as a cornerstone of Western political theory, ethics, and metaphysics for over two millennia.'),(9,'The Body A Guide for Occupants','Bill Bryson','Health',2,1,'no',0,'biology, anatomy, fitness','Bill Bryson takes readers on an entertaining head-to-toe tour of the marvelous and often baffling machine that each of us inhabits. Combining sharp wit with extensive interviews and medical history, he explains how the body\'s trillions of cells cooperate seamlessly to keep us alive, heal wounds, fight off microscopic invaders, and process the world around us.\n\nFrom the mysteries of the human brain and the hidden ecosystems of microbes living on our skin to the history of surgical breakthroughs and the mechanics of sleep, aging, and immunity, the book turns complex anatomy and physiology into an accessible, awe-inspiring narrative.'),(10,'1984','George Orwell','Fiction',2,2,'yes',2,'dystopia, surveillance, totalitarianism','In the bleak superstate of Oceania, the Party rules with absolute authority under the omnipresent gaze of Big Brother. Winston Smith, a low-ranking official at the Ministry of Truth, spends his days rewriting historical records to match the Party\'s ever-shifting propaganda, while secretly harboring rebellious thoughts and a forbidden desire for intellectual freedom.\n\nWhen Winston begins an illicit love affair with a fellow worker named Julia, the two attempt to carve out a private existence beyond the reach of telescreens and the Thought Police. Orwell\'s classic remains a terrifyingly prescient portrait of totalitarianism, mass surveillance, linguistic manipulation (\"Newspeak\"), and the systematic erasure of objective truth.'),(11,'Dune','Frank Herbert','Sci-Fi',3,0,'no',0,'desert, space, arrakis, classic','Set thousands of years in the future amidst a feudal interstellar empire, the story centers on young Paul Atreides as his noble family accepts stewardship of the desert planet Arrakis. Inhospitable and sparsely populated, Arrakis is the sole source of melange—the \"spice\"—a substance capable of extending human life, unlocking prescient consciousness, and making interstellar navigation possible.\n\nWhen a devastating betrayal orchestrated by rival House Harkonnen and the Padishah Emperor shatters House Atreides, Paul and his mother Jessica flee into the deep desert and seek refuge among the native Fremen. Blending ecology, religion, political intrigue, and messianic prophecy, the novel stands as a landmark of modern science fiction.'),(12,'Atomic Habits','James Clear','Self-Help',3,0,'no',0,'habits, productivity, growth, psychology','James Clear argues that lasting personal transformation does not require radical, overnight upheavals, but rather the compound effect of tiny, consistent adjustments—getting just one percent better each day. Drawing on insights from biology, psychology, and neuroscience, the book breaks down the anatomy of a habit into a four-step loop: cue, craving, response, and reward.\n\nFrom this foundation, Clear introduces the Four Laws of Behavior Change: make it obvious, make it attractive, make it easy, and make it satisfying. Packed with actionable strategies like habit stacking, environment design, and identity-based goal setting, it provides a practical operating manual for building productive routines and dismantling destructive ones.'),(13,'The Pragmatic Programmer','David Thomas','Technology',2,0,'no',0,'coding, software, professional, career','Cutting through faddish frameworks and language-specific trivia, this classic guide examines what it truly means to approach software development as a disciplined craft. Through a collection of self-contained topics, metaphors, and practical tips—such as \"Don\'t Repeat Yourself\" (DRY), \"Tracer Bullets,\" and avoiding \"Software Rot\"—the authors illustrate how pragmatic engineers think and solve problems.\n\nThe book covers everything from personal responsibility and career growth to architectural decoupling, automation, plain-text workflows, and pragmatic testing. It equips developers with timeless mental models to write adaptable, resilient code in an ever-changing technological landscape.'),(14,'Project Hail Mary','Andy Weir','Sci-Fi',3,0,'no',0,'space, survival, aliens, science','Ryland Grace wakes up from a coma on a spacecraft light-years from home with no memory of who he is or how he got there. His two crewmates are dead, and as his memories slowly return in fragments, he realizes he is a junior high school teacher turned molecular biologist—and the sole survivor of a desperate, one-way mission to save Earth from an extinction-level stellar microorganism.\n\nStranded in the Tau Ceti system, Grace must rely on his scientific ingenuity, improvised experiments, and an unexpected alien ally to solve an astronomical puzzle before humanity freezes to death. It is a fast-paced survival story driven by hard science, humor, and interstellar friendship.'),(15,'Shoe Dog','Phil Knight','Biography',2,0,'no',0,'business, nike, memoir, entrepreneurship','Fresh out of business school in 1962, a twenty-four-year-old Phil Knight borrowed fifty dollars from his father and launched a startup with a simple mission: import high-quality, low-cost running shoes from Japan. Selling sneakers out of the trunk of his Plymouth Valiant during that first year, Knight laid the foundations for what would eventually become Nike.\n\nIn this candid memoir, Knight recounts the chaotic early decades of the company—navigating razor-thin margins, hostile bankers, ruthless competitors, and a sudden betrayal by his Japanese supplier—alongside the band of eccentric misfits and running enthusiasts who built one of the world\'s most recognizable brands from the ground up.'),(16,'The Silent Patient','Alex Michaelides','Thriller',2,0,'no',0,'psychological, mystery, murder, suspense','Alicia Berenson\'s life appears enviable: she is a celebrated painter married to an in-demand fashion photographer, living in a grand home overlooking a park in London. One evening, her husband Gabriel returns home late from a shoot, and Alicia shoots him five times in the face—and then never speaks another word.\n\nHer refusal to speak or explain herself turns a domestic tragedy into a gripping public mystery, landing Alicia in the Grove, a secure forensic unit in North London. Theo Faber, a criminal psychotherapist who has long been fixated on Alicia\'s case, secures a position at the facility determined to unravel her silence, unaware that his obsession will consume his own life.'),(17,'Tomorrow, and Tomorrow, and Tomorrow','Gabrielle Zevin','Fiction',2,0,'no',0,'gaming, relationships, drama, love','On a freezing day in December, college students Sam Masur and Sadie Green spot each other on a crowded subway platform in Cambridge, Massachusetts. Having bonded years earlier in a hospital gaming room before a painful falling-out, this chance reunion reignites their creative partnership and launches them into a decades-long journey as video game designers.\n\nBefore they reach twenty-five, their first blockbuster game, Ichigo, brings them fame, artistic fulfillment, and wealth, alongside fierce creative clashes, unspoken jealousies, and personal tragedy. Spanning thirty years from Massachusetts to California and the virtual worlds in between, the novel explores the nature of play, creative collaboration, disability, and the enduring forms of love that defy traditional romance.'),(18,'Fourth Wing','Rebecca Yarros','Fantasy',3,0,'no',0,'dragons, magic, romance, war','Twenty-year-old Violet Sorrengail expected to spend her life quietly among books and historical archives in the Scribe Quadrant. Instead, her formidable mother—the commanding general of Navarre—orders her to join the hundreds of candidates striving to become the elite of their kingdom: dragon riders at Basgiath War College.\n\nFragile in stature and prone to injury, Violet enters a deadly training ground where cadets are encouraged to thin the ranks by any means necessary, and where dragons refuse to bond with weak humans. Worse still, Xaden Riorson, the ruthless wingleader of the Riders Quadrant and the son of a rebellion leader executed by Violet\'s mother, has every reason to want her dead.'),(19,'The Psychology of Money','Morgan Housel','Finance',2,0,'no',0,'wealth, mindset, economics, investing','Doing well with money has surprisingly little to do with how smart you are and almost everything to do with how you behave. Financial writer Morgan Housel argues that investing and personal finance are typically taught as math-based fields governed by formulas and data, when in the real world people make financial decisions at the dinner table, driven by personal history, pride, fear, and social comparison.\n\nThrough nineteen short stories, Housel explores the strange ways people think about wealth, the difference between getting rich and staying rich, the power of compounding, the role of luck and risk, and how defining \"enough\" is the ultimate key to financial peace of mind.'),(20,'Dark Matter','Blake Crouch','Thriller',2,0,'no',0,'multiverse, suspense, science, physics','Jason Dessen, a former quantum physicist who gave up a promising research career to become a college professor and devoted family man, is walking home through the streets of Chicago one night when he is abducted at gunpoint. Drugged and knocked unconscious, he wakes up strapped to a gurney surrounded by strangers congratulating him on his return.\n\nIn this reality, his wife is not his wife, his son was never born, and Jason himself is a celebrated scientific genius who has just achieved the impossible: building a cube capable of traversing the multiverse. To get back to the family he loves, Jason must navigate a labyrinth of alternate realities and confront the darkest version of himself.'),(21,'The Midnight Library','Matt Haig','Fiction',2,0,'no',0,'choices, life, philosophy, regrets','Overwhelmed by depression, loneliness, and the weight of her perceived failures, thirty-five-year-old Nora Seed decides to end her life. At the stroke of midnight, however, she finds herself neither in heaven nor in oblivion, but standing inside a vast library whose shelves stretch endlessly in every direction.\n\nGuided by her childhood school librarian, Mrs. Elm, Nora learns that each book in the Midnight Library offers a portal into a parallel life she could have lived had she made a different choice—whether staying with an ex-partner, pursuing her dream as an Olympic swimmer, becoming a rock star, or studying glaciers in the Arctic. As she steps into these alternate realities, Nora must discover what truly makes life worth living.'),(22,'System Design Interview','Alex Xu','Technology',2,0,'no',0,'interviews, architecture, scaling, backend','System design interviews are notoriously open-ended and intimidating, testing a software engineer\'s ability to architect large-scale distributed systems under pressure with no single \"correct\" answer. Alex Xu demystifies the process by providing a structured, four-step framework that walks candidates from gathering requirements and estimating scale to deep-diving into components and resolving bottlenecks.\n\nBacked by clear architectural diagrams, the book works through real-world design problems step by step—including building a URL shortener, a rate limiter, a consistent hashing ring, a distributed key-value store, a news feed system, a chat application, and a video streaming platform.'),(23,'The Great Gatsby','F. Scott Fitzgerald','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'jazz, wealth, society, romance','In the summer of 1922, Nick Carraway moves from the Midwest to West Egg, Long Island, renting a modest house next door to the palatial estate of Jay Gatsby, a mysterious millionaire famous for throwing extravagant, champagne-soaked parties every weekend. Drawn into the glittering orbit of the ultra-wealthy, Nick eventually learns that Gatsby\'s entire fortune and spectacle exist for a single purpose: to win back Nick\'s cousin, Daisy Buchanan, whom Gatsby loved five years earlier.\n\nBeneath the jazz-age glamour of Long Island and Manhattan lies a world of carelessness, moral decay, and entrenched class privilege. Fitzgerald\'s masterpiece captures both the intoxicating romance and the tragic hollowness of the American Dream.'),(24,'Moby Dick','Herman Melville','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'whales, ocean, obsession, adventure','Seeking adventure and escape from life on land, a young sailor who calls himself Ishmael signs aboard the Pequod, a Nantucket whaling ship, alongside his newfound friend Queequeg, a tattooed Polynesian harpooner. Once at sea, the crew discovers that their peg-legged commander, Captain Ahab, has no interest in a conventional commercial voyage—he is consumed by a monomaniacal desire for vengeance against Moby Dick, the colossal white sperm whale that bit off his leg.\n\nBlending thrilling maritime action, cetological lore, and Shakespearean tragedy, Melville turns the Pequod\'s doomed pursuit across the globe into a profound meditation on fate, human obsession, nature\'s indifference, and the limits of knowledge.'),(25,'War and Peace','Leo Tolstoy','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'russia, history, war, society','Beginning in the glittering salons of Saint Petersburg in 1805 and culminating in the devastating French invasion of 1812, Leo Tolstoy\'s monumental epic follows five aristocratic Russian families as their private lives collide with the sweep of Napoleonic history. At the heart of the narrative are three unforgettable figures: Pierre Bezukhov, an awkward, idealistic bastard son searching for spiritual meaning; Prince Andrei Bolkonsky, a cynical intellectual seeking glory on the battlefield; and Natasha Rostova, a vibrant young woman navigating love and heartbreak.\n\nMoving seamlessly between intimate drawing rooms and the chaos of Austerlitz and Borodino, Tolstoy weaves narrative fiction with philosophical reflections on free will, leadership, and the forces that drive human history.'),(26,'Crime and Punishment','Fyodor Dostoevsky','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'guilt, psychology, murder, morality','Living in a cramped, sweltering garret in nineteenth-century Saint Petersburg, impoverished former student Rodion Raskolnikov becomes consumed by a radical theory: that extraordinary individuals—like Napoleon—possess the moral right to step over conventional law if their actions serve a higher purpose. To test himself and escape poverty, he resolves to murder an unscrupulous, elderly pawnbroker.\n\nThe crime, however, goes horribly awry, and Raskolnikov is immediately plunged into a feverish nightmare of paranoia, isolation, and psychological torment. Pursued by the shrewd magistrate Porfiry Petrovich and drawn toward the quiet faith of Sonia, a young woman forced into prostitution to feed her family, Raskolnikov must confront the inescapable weight of human conscience.'),(27,'Jane Eyre','Charlotte Brontë','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'romance, gothic, independence','Orphaned as a baby, Jane Eyre endures a cruel childhood first at the hands of her resentful aunt, Mrs. Reed, and then at the harsh Lowood School, where poverty and typhus test her spirit. Refusing to let mistreatment extinguish her fierce sense of dignity and moral independence, Jane grows into a composed young woman and accepts a position as governess at Thornfield Hall.\n\nThere, she becomes teacher to the young ward of the brooding, enigmatic Edward Rochester. Though separated by wealth and social station, Jane and Rochester are drawn together by an intense intellectual kinship—until a terrifying secret locked in Thornfield\'s attic shatters their wedding day and forces Jane to choose between passion and self-respect.'),(28,'The Catcher in the Rye','J.D. Salinger','Fiction',2,0,'no',0,'youth, rebellion, angst, new york','Expelled from Pencey Prep, his fourth private school, for failing nearly all his classes, sixteen-year-old Holden Caulfield decides to leave campus a few days early rather than face his parents right away. Wandering alone through New York City over a bleak December weekend, Holden checks into a seedy hotel, visits nightclubs, reconnects with old acquaintances, and sneaks home to visit his beloved younger sister, Phoebe.\n\nBehind Holden\'s cynical tirades against the \"phonies\" of the adult world lies a deeply vulnerable teenager grieving the death of his younger brother Allie and terrified of the corruption that comes with growing up. Salinger\'s novel remains a defining portrait of adolescent alienation, grief, and the longing to preserve innocence.'),(29,'Animal Farm','George Orwell','Sci-Fi',2,0,'no',0,'dystopia, rebellion, politics, satire','Exhausted by the neglect and exploitation of their drunken owner, Mr. Jones, the overworked animals of Manor Farm rise up in rebellion and drive the humans off the land. Inspired by the vision of the prize boar Old Major, they rename the property Animal Farm and establish a society built on the Seven Commandments of Animalism, the most important being that \"All animals are equal.\"\n\nInitially, the farm prospers under the leadership of the pigs Snowball and Napoleon, but before long, Napoleon uses a pack of trained attack dogs to seize absolute power. Through this razor-sharp fable of the Russian Revolution and the rise of Stalinism, Orwell demonstrates how revolutionary ideals can be systematically corrupted into a tyranny indistinguishable from the one they replaced.'),(30,'Brave New World','Aldous Huxley','Sci-Fi',2,0,'no',0,'dystopia, future, technology, control','In the year 632 A.F. (\"After Ford\"), the World State has achieved total planetary stability. Humans are no longer born to mothers, but decanted on assembly lines in Hatcheries and genetically engineered into a rigid caste system ranging from intellectual Alphas to menial Epsilons. Through sleep-learning conditioning (\"hypnopaedia\"), mandatory promiscuity, and a mood-altering drug called soma, pain, art, religion, and deep emotion have been eradicated in favor of effortless consumer pleasure.\n\nWhen Bernard Marx, a discontented Alpha psychologist, visits a Savage Reservation in New Mexico and brings back John—a young man raised on the works of Shakespeare outside the World State—the collision between untamed human passion and engineered utopia exposes the terrifying cost of a painless society.'),(31,'The Hobbit','J.R.R. Tolkien','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'dragons, adventure, magic, middle-earth','Bilbo Baggins is a respectable, comfort-loving hobbit who enjoys six meals a day and has zero interest in adventures. His quiet life in Bag End is turned upside down when the wizard Gandalf and a company of thirteen dwarves led by Thorin Oakenshield arrive on his doorstep, recruiting Bilbo as the \"burglar\" for a perilous expedition across Middle-earth to the Lonely Mountain.\n\nTheir goal is to reclaim the ancient dwarf kingdom of Erebor and its vast treasure from Smaug, a cunning and fearsome dragon. Along the journey through troll-infested woods, goblin tunnels beneath the Misty Mountains, and the dark depths of Mirkwood, Bilbo stumbles upon a mysterious golden ring in the cave of Gollum—an encounter that forever alters the fate of Middle-earth.'),(32,'Harry Potter and the Sorcerer\'s Stone','J.K. Rowling','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'magic, school, wizards, fantasy','Having spent ten miserable years sleeping in a cupboard under the stairs at his cruel aunt and uncle\'s house on Privet Drive, Harry Potter has never had a reason to celebrate his birthday. On his eleventh birthday, however, a giant named Rubeus Hagrid delivers a letter that reveals the truth: Harry\'s late parents were wizards, Harry himself is famous throughout a hidden magical world for surviving the attack of the dark wizard Lord Voldemort, and he has been accepted to Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry.\n\nSorted into Gryffindor house alongside his new best friends Ron Weasley and Hermione Granger, Harry learns to fly on a broomstick, brew potions, and navigate a magical castle—while uncovering a plot to steal a legendary alchemical object hidden deep within the school.'),(33,'The Fellowship of the Ring','J.R.R. Tolkien','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'elves, rings, quest, darkness','For millennia, the One Ring—forged in secret by the Dark Lord Sauron to rule all of Middle-earth—has been lost to history, passing unexpectedly into the hands of the hobbit Bilbo Baggins and subsequently to his young heir, Frodo. When the wizard Gandalf confirms the Ring\'s true nature, Frodo must flee the peaceful Shire pursued by Sauron\'s terrifying undead servants, the Ringwraiths.\n\nAt the Council of Elrond in Rivendell, it becomes clear that the Ring cannot be hidden or used for good; it must be cast back into the fires of Mount Doom in the heart of Mordor. Joined by eight companions—representing Men, Elves, Dwarves, and Hobbits—Frodo sets out as the Ring-bearer in the Fellowship of the Ring, undertaking a desperate journey as darkness gathers across the world.'),(34,'Foundation','Isaac Asimov','Sci-Fi',2,0,'no',0,'empire, space, future, psychology','For twelve thousand years, the Galactic Empire has ruled millions of worlds across the Milky Way with seemingly unshakable authority. Only Hari Seldon, the creator of the revolutionary science of \"psychohistory\"—which uses advanced mathematics and crowd psychology to predict the future behavior of massive populations—can see that the Empire is in terminal decline and will soon collapse into thirty thousand years of barbarism.\n\nUnable to prevent the fall, Seldon devises a bold plan to shorten the coming Dark Age to a single millennium by establishing the Foundation: a colony of scientists and scholars exiled to the remote planet Terminus at the edge of the galaxy. Over generations, the fledgling colony must survive aggressive neighbors and political crises guided only by Seldon\'s pre-calculated historical trajectory.'),(35,'Neuromancer','William Gibson','Sci-Fi',2,0,'no',0,'cyberpunk, hacking, ai, future','Henry Dorsett Case was once one of the sharpest \"console cowboys\" in the Sprawl, jacking his consciousness directly into the global data matrix to steal corporate secrets. After he made the mistake of stealing from his own employers, they retaliated by crippling his nervous system with a Russian mycotoxin, leaving him trapped in the physical world of Chiba City\'s neon-lit underworld.\n\nJust as Case hits rock bottom, he is recruited by Armitage, a shadowy ex-military operative, and Molly Millions, a street samurai with mirrored lenses inset over her eyes and retractable razors beneath her fingernails. In exchange for repairing his neural damage, Case must pull off an impossible run against an orbital artificial intelligence whose motives reach far beyond corporate espionage.'),(36,'The Martian','Andy Weir','Sci-Fi',2,0,'no',0,'mars, survival, science, space','Six days after becoming one of the first humans to walk on Mars, astronaut Mark Watney is caught in a ferocious dust storm that forces his Ares 3 crewmates to abort their mission and evacuate the planet. Struck by a sheared communications antenna and swept away in the gale with his biomonitor flatlining, Watney is presumed dead—until he wakes up alone on a barren red world with no way to signal Earth.\n\nWith meager rations, a damaged habitat, and the next manned Mars mission four years and thousands of kilometers away, Watney refuses to give up. Armed with his training as a botanist and mechanical engineer, plus a relentless sense of humor, he sets out to grow potatoes in Martian soil and outsmart one life-threatening failure after another.'),(37,'The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo','Stieg Larsson','Thriller',2,0,'no',0,'mystery, murder, hacker, sweden','Disgraced after losing a libel suit against a corrupt financier, investigative journalist Mikael Blomkvist accepts an unusual assignment from Henrik Vanger, the aging patriarch of one of Sweden\'s wealthiest industrial families. Forty years earlier, Henrik\'s sixteen-year-old niece, Harriet, vanished without a trace from the family\'s private island during a gathering, and Henrik is convinced she was murdered by a member of his own dysfunctional clan.\n\nTo assist in his investigation, Blomkvist is paired with Lisbeth Salander, a fiercely independent twenty-four-year-old computer hacker with a photographic memory and a traumatic past. As the unlikely duo digs into the Vanger family archives, they unearth a horrifying trail of sadism, corporate corruption, and serial murder.'),(38,'Gone Girl','Gillian Flynn','Thriller',2,0,'no',0,'marriage, disappearance, twist, psychological','On the morning of their fifth wedding anniversary in North Carthage, Missouri, Nick Dunne returns home to find his front door ajar, signs of a struggle in the living room, and his beautiful, sharp-witted wife, Amy, missing. As the police investigation and a national media frenzy descend on the town, Nick\'s detached demeanor, financial struggles, and series of lies quickly transform him from sympathetic husband into the prime suspect.\n\nTold in alternating chapters between Nick\'s present-day perspective and entries from Amy\'s past diary, the narrative peels back the layers of a toxic modern marriage. Just when the truth seems clear, a shocking mid-novel twist upends everything the reader thought they knew about both husband and wife.'),(39,'The Da Vinci Code','Dan Brown','Mystery',2,0,'no',0,'religion, history, art, cryptography','While in Paris on business, Harvard symbologist Robert Langdon receives an urgent late-night summons to the Louvre Museum, where the elderly curator, Jacques Saunière, has been murdered inside the Grand Gallery. Before dying, Saunière arranged his own body in the pose of Leonardo da Vinci\'s Vitruvian Man and scrawled a baffling series of ciphers and symbols across the floor in invisible ink.\n\nJoined by gifted French cryptologist Sophie Neveu—who discovers she is both Saunière\'s estranged granddaughter and a target of the investigation—Langdon realizes the clues point to the Priory of Sion, a clandestine society whose past members included Sir Isaac Newton and Da Vinci. Racing from Paris to London while pursued by police and a fanatical assassin, they uncover a centuries-old religious secret.'),(40,'And Then There Were None','Agatha Christie','Mystery',2,0,'no',0,'island, murder, classic, suspense','Ten strangers—a reckless playboy, a troubled governess, a retired general, a stern judge, a nervous doctor, a pious spinster, a former police inspector, a mercenary soldier, and two household servants—are lured to Soldier Island, a rocky estate off the coast of Devon, by an enigmatic host named U.N. Owen. Upon arrival, they find their host absent, and during their first dinner together, a recorded voice accuses each guest of having gotten away with murder in the past.\n\nCut off from the mainland by a violent storm and with no boat in sight, the guests begin dying one by one in ways that mirror the verses of the framed nursery rhyme \"Ten Little Soldier Boys\" hanging in every bedroom. With no one else on the island, the dwindling survivors realize the killer must be one of them.'),(41,'Steve Jobs','Walter Isaacson','Biography',2,0,'no',0,'apple, technology, business, life','Based on more than forty interviews with Steve Jobs conducted over two years—as well as interviews with over a hundred family members, friends, adversaries, competitors, and colleagues—Walter Isaacson presents an unflinching portrait of the co-founder of Apple. Jobs\'s relentless drive for perfection, countercultural intuition, and notorious \"reality distortion field\" revolutionized six industries: personal computers, animated movies, music, phones, tablet computing, and digital publishing.\n\nIsaacson traces Jobs\'s life from his childhood adoption in Silicon Valley and his early days building circuit boards in a garage with Steve Wozniak, through his ouster from Apple, his years at NeXT and Pixar, and his triumphant return to build the iMac, iPod, iPhone, and iPad.'),(42,'The Diary of a Young Girl','Anne Frank','Biography',2,0,'no',0,'history, wwii, holocaust, poignant','In July 1942, as Nazi persecution of Jews intensified across occupied Holland, thirteen-year-old Anne Frank and her family went into hiding in the \"Secret Annex\"—a concealed set of rooms behind her father Otto\'s office building in Amsterdam. Joined by another family, the van Pels, and a dentist named Fritz Pfeffer, eight people lived in cramped silence for over two years, cut off from the outside world until their betrayal and arrest in August 1944.\n\nAddressing her entries to an imaginary friend named \"Kitty,\" Anne chronicled the daily tensions of life in hiding alongside her own budding adolescence, philosophical reflections, fears of discovery, and unshakeable belief in human goodness. Her diary remains one of the most moving and vital historical documents of the Holocaust.'),(43,'Thinking, Fast and Slow','Daniel Kahneman','Psychology',2,0,'no',0,'mind, decisions, economics, behavior','Nobel Prize-winning psychologist Daniel Kahneman synthesizes decades of groundbreaking research—much of it conducted with his late collaborator Amos Tversky—to explain how the human mind processes information and makes judgments. He introduces two modes of thought: \"System 1,\" which operates automatically, quickly, and emotionally with little effort, and \"System 2,\" which allocates attention to slow, deliberate, and logical mental calculations.\n\nWhile System 1 allows us to navigate daily life efficiently, its reliance on mental shortcuts (heuristics) leaves us vulnerable to systematic cognitive biases—from anchoring and overconfidence to loss aversion and the framing effect. The book transforms how readers understand human rationality, economics, and everyday decision-making.'),(44,'The Power of Habit','Charles Duhigg','Self-Help',2,0,'no',0,'habits, change, routine, science','Drawing on hundreds of academic studies and interviews with executives, neuroscientists, and social leaders, Pulitzer Prize-winning reporter Charles Duhigg explores why habits exist and how they can be re-engineered. At the core of every habit is a neurological three-part pattern—a cue, a routine, and a reward—that allows the brain to conserve mental energy by turning complex behaviors into automatic scripts.\n\nDuhigg illustrates how this \"Habit Loop\" operates across three levels: in individual lives, inside successful organizations like Alcoa and Starbucks through \"keystone habits,\" and within broad social movements such as the Montgomery Bus Boycott. By learning to identify the cues and rewards driving a behavior, readers gain a practical blueprint for replacing old routines.'),(45,'Outliers','Malcolm Gladwell','Sociology',2,0,'no',0,'success, culture, timing, psychology','We often attribute extraordinary success strictly to individual merit—believing that the brightest, most ambitious people naturally rise to the top through sheer willpower. Malcolm Gladwell challenges this myth by looking beyond the personal traits of high achievers to examine their environment: their family backgrounds, their birthplaces, their cultural legacies, and the specific historical moments in which they came of age.\n\nFrom why the vast majority of elite Canadian hockey players are born in the first few months of the year, to how Bill Gates and the Beatles benefited from rare opportunities to accumulate 10,000 hours of practice, Gladwell reveals the hidden structural advantages and cultural patterns that shape world-class success.'),(46,'In Cold Blood','Truman Capote','History',2,0,'no',0,'true crime, murder, journalism, kansas','On November 15, 1959, in the quiet rural community of Holcomb, Kansas, four members of the prosperous Clutter family were savagely murdered by shotgun blasts in their home for a handful of dollars. There were no apparent motives and almost no clues. Intrigued by a brief newspaper clipping about the crime, Truman Capote traveled to Kansas alongside his childhood friend Harper Lee to interview townspeople, investigators, and eventually the killers themselves.\n\nReconstructing the crime from alternating perspectives—moving between the victims\' final day, the shockwave of fear across Holcomb, and the wandering flight of ex-convicts Perry Smith and Dick Hickock—Capote pioneered the \"nonfiction novel,\" blending journalistic rigor with the atmospheric suspense of literary fiction.'),(47,'Into Thin Air','Jon Krakauer','History',2,0,'no',0,'everest, survival, disaster, climbing','In March 1996, journalist and experienced mountaineer Jon Krakauer traveled to the Himalayas on assignment for Outside magazine to report on the growing commercialization of Mount Everest. Joining a guided expedition led by renowned New Zealand climber Rob Hall, Krakauer set out to examine how wealthy amateurs were paying tens of thousands of dollars to be escorted to the top of the world.\n\nOn May 10, Krakauer reached the 29,028-foot summit, but as climbers began their descent in the thin air of the \"Death Zone,\" a rogue blizzard slammed into the mountain. Through exhausted, oxygen-starved eyes, Krakauer recounts the cascading series of small delays, miscommunications, and extreme weather that turned the expedition into one of the deadliest days in Everest history.'),(48,'The Alchemist','Paulo Coelho','Fiction',2,0,'no',0,'journey, destiny, dreams, philosophy','Santiago, an Andalusian shepherd boy who left a seminary to travel the countryside with his flock, becomes troubled by a recurring dream about a treasure hidden near the Egyptian pyramids. After encounters with a Romani fortune-teller and a mysterious old man claiming to be Melchizedek, the King of Salem, Santiago sells his sheep and sets sail across the Strait of Gibraltar to pursue his \"Personal Legend.\"\n\nRobbed in Tangier, working in a crystal merchant\'s shop, crossing the Sahara with a desert caravan, and studying under an enigmatic alchemist at the Al-Fayoum oasis, Santiago learns to read the omens of the world and listen to his heart. Paulo Coelho\'s modern fable celebrates self-discovery, spiritual perseverance, and the wisdom found in the journey itself.'),(49,'The Kite Runner','Khaled Hosseini','Fiction',2,0,'no',0,'afghanistan, redemption, friendship, war','Growing up in a wealthy neighborhood of Kabul in the 1970s, twelve-year-old Amir is desperate to win the approval of his charismatic father, Baba, by triumphing in the city\'s annual kite-fighting tournament. His constant companion and most loyal supporter is Hassan, the son of Baba\'s Hazara servant—a boy who would do anything for Amir. On the afternoon of the tournament, however, a horrific act of violence in a back alley shatters their bond, and Amir\'s cowardice drives a permanent wedge of guilt between them.\n\nAfter the Soviet invasion forces Amir and his father to flee to California as refugees, Amir builds a new life in America, only to receive a phone call decades later from an old family friend offering him \"a way to be good again\" by returning to Taliban-ruled Afghanistan.'),(50,'The Book Thief','Markus Zusak','Fiction',2,0,'no',0,'wwii, death, books, germany','Set in 1939 Nazi Germany and narrated by a weary, compassionate Death, the story follows nine-year-old Liesel Meminger after she is sent to live with foster parents Hans and Rosa Hubermann on Himmel Street in the town of Molching. Traumatized by the death of her younger brother on the journey there, Liesel clings to the one object she picked up in the snow by his graveside: The Grave Digger\'s Handbook.\n\nWith the patient help of her accordion-playing foster father, Liesel learns to read and begins rescuing books from Nazi bonfires and the mayor\'s library. When the Hubermanns risk everything to hide Max Vandenburg, a Jewish fist-fighter, in their basement, words and stories become a lifeline of humanity amidst the terror of war and bombing raids.'),(51,'The Hunger Games','Suzanne Collins','Sci-Fi',2,0,'no',0,'dystopia, survival, games, rebellion','In the ruins of a place once known as North America lies the nation of Panem, where a wealthy, decadent Capitol holds twelve surrounding districts in submission. As punishment for a past uprising, each district is forced every year to send one boy and one girl between the ages of twelve and eighteen to participate in the Hunger Games—a nationally televised event in which \"tributes\" fight to the death inside a controlled outdoor arena until a single survivor remains.\n\nWhen sixteen-year-old Katniss Everdeen steps forward to take her younger sister Prim\'s place in the reaping for District 12, she is thrust into the arena alongside her fellow tribute, Peeta Mellark. Armed with her hunting skills and survival instincts, Katniss must weigh survival against humanity in a game designed to break both.'),(52,'Catch-22','Joseph Heller','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'satire, war, military, absurd','Stationed on the small island of Pianosa off the coast of Italy during World War II, U.S. Army Air Forces bombardier Captain John Yossarian has a single overriding goal: to stay alive while his ambitious commander, Colonel Cathcart, continually raises the number of combat missions the men must fly before they can go home. Desperate to be grounded, Yossarian appeals to the squadron doctor, only to encounter the inescapable military rule known as Catch-22.\n\nAccording to Catch-22, a man who flies combat missions must be crazy and can be grounded—but if he asks to be grounded, that proves he is rational and sane, meaning he must keep flying. Through a cast of profiteers, bureaucrats, and traumatized airmen, Joseph Heller crafts a blistering tragicomic satire on the absurdity of war and institutional madness.'),(53,'Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets','J.K. Rowling','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'magic, wizards, hogwarts, chamber, mystery','After a miserable summer with the Dursleys—punctuated by the arrival of a frantic house-elf named Dobby who warns him that terrible danger awaits him at school—Harry Potter is rescued by Ron Weasley and his brothers in a flying Ford Anglia. Returning to Hogwarts for his second year, Harry soon hears an eerie, disembodied voice echoing through the castle walls, followed by an ominous message written in blood declaring that the legendary Chamber of Secrets has been opened.\n\nAs students and ghosts are found petrified one by one, fear grips the school that the \"Heir of Slytherin\" has unleashed an ancient monster to purge Hogwarts of Muggle-born wizards. Together with Ron and Hermione, Harry must uncover a fifty-year-old secret before the school is forced to close forever.'),(54,'Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban','J.K. Rowling','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'magic, dementors, azkaban, sirius black','As Harry Potter prepares to start his third year at Hogwarts, the wizarding world is in an uproar: Sirius Black, a notorious mass murderer and devoted follower of Lord Voldemort, has accomplished the impossible by escaping from the wizard prison of Azkaban. Worse still, Ministry officials have reason to believe that Black\'s primary target is Harry himself.\n\nUpon arriving at school, Harry and his classmates find the grounds patrolled by the Dementors of Azkaban—soul-sucking guards whose icy presence affects Harry more deeply than anyone else. Aided by their new Defense Against the Dark Arts teacher, Professor Lupin, and a magical parchment called the Marauder\'s Map, Harry uncovers the painful truth about his parents\' betrayal.'),(55,'The Two Towers','J.R.R. Tolkien','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'middle-earth, ring, frodo, fellowship, rohan','Following the ambush at Amon Hen, the death of Boromir, and the fall of Gandalf in Moria, the Fellowship of the Ring is fractured into three separate paths. While Frodo Baggins and his loyal companion Samwise Gamgee press onward alone toward the dark borders of Mordor—forced to rely on the treacherous creature Gollum as their guide—Aragorn, Legolas, and Gimli race across the plains of Rohan to rescue the captured hobbits Merry and Pippin from a band of Uruk-hai.\n\nAs the wizard Saruman unleashes a massive army from the fortress of Isengard to wipe out the horse-lords of Rohan at the Battle of Helm\'s Deep, Merry and Pippin rouse the ancient, tree-like Ents of Fangorn Forest. Across Middle-earth, the forces of resistance rally against the growing shadow of the Two Towers.'),(56,'The Return of the King','J.R.R. Tolkien','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'middle-earth, gondor, battle, ring, finale','As Sauron launches his final, overwhelming assault against the realm of Men, Gandalf and Pippin ride to Minas Tirith, the besieged capital of Gondor, where the Steward Denethor is slipping into madness and despair. To save the city, Aragorn must claim his ancient birthright as the heir of Isildur, walk the haunted Paths of the Dead, and lead the armies of the West in a desperate stand before the Black Gate of Mordor to draw Sauron\'s gaze.\n\nMeanwhile, deep inside the volcanic wastelands of Mordor, Frodo and Sam—exhausted, starving, and stalked by Gollum and the monstrous spider Shelob—make their final, agonizing climb up the slopes of Mount Doom, where the corrupting power of the One Ring reaches its peak.'),(57,'A Game of Thrones','George R.R. Martin','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'westeros, dragons, politics, stark, lannister','In the Seven Kingdoms of Westeros, where seasons can last for decades, Lord Eddard \"Ned\" Stark is reluctant to leave his northern stronghold of Winterfell when his old friend, King Robert Baratheon, rides north to name him Hand of the King. The previous Hand died under suspicious circumstances, and entering the viper\'s nest of the royal court in King\'s Landing soon exposes Ned to a web of incest, treason, and assassination plotted by the wealthy House Lannister.\n\nMeanwhile, across the Narrow Sea, the exiled Princess Daenerys Targaryen is wed to a powerful Dothraki warlord in a bid to reclaim her family\'s stolen Iron Throne, while in the icy wastes beyond the Wall in the far North, Ned\'s bastard son Jon Snow joins the Night\'s Watch just as an ancient, supernatural threat awakens after thousands of years.'),(58,'Catching Fire','Suzanne Collins','Sci-Fi',2,0,'no',0,'dystopia, rebellion, survival, panem','Against all odds, Katniss Everdeen and Peeta Mellark survived the 74th Hunger Games by forcing the Capitol to declare them both victors with a handful of poisonous berries. While Katniss only wanted to save Peeta and return home to her family in District 12, President Snow views her final act in the arena as a dangerous spark of defiance that is already igniting unrest across the oppressed districts of Panem.\n\nAs Katniss and Peeta embark on the mandatory Victory Tour, they witness firsthand the brutal crackdowns taking place in the districts. To crush the burgeoning rebellion and eliminate Katniss as a symbol of hope, the Capitol announces a cruel twist for the 75th Hunger Games—the third \"Quarter Quell\"—which will reap its tributes directly from the surviving pool of past victors.'),(59,'The Lightning Thief','Rick Riordan','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'mythology, gods, percy jackson, quest, monsters','Twelve-year-old Percy Jackson is used to getting kicked out of boarding schools, struggling with dyslexia and ADHD, and experiencing bizarre accidents he can\'t explain. Everything changes during a class field trip when his pre-algebra teacher transforms into a winged monster from Greek mythology and tries to vaporize him, forcing Percy\'s mother and his best friend Grover to rush him to Camp Half-Blood, a secret training ground on Long Island.\n\nThere, Percy learns that the gods of Mount Olympus are very much alive—and that he is a demigod son of Poseidon, the god of the sea. Worse yet, Zeus\'s master lightning bolt has been stolen, Percy is the prime suspect, and he has just ten days to travel across America to the gates of the Underworld in Los Angeles to find the real thief and prevent a catastrophic war among the gods.'),(60,'The Last Wish','Andrzej Sapkowski','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'witcher, monsters, geralt, magic, fantasy','Geralt of Rivia is a witcher—a professional monster hunter mutated since childhood through alchemical trials and rigorous sword training to possess superhuman reflexes, heightened senses, and magic signs. Riding across a war-torn Continent where humans are frequently far more monstrous than the beasts lurking in the woods, Geralt tries to maintain a strict code of neutrality while earning his coin.\n\nStructured as an interconnected frame narrative while Geralt recovers from grave wounds at the Temple of Melitele, this collection weaves together dark, subversive retellings of classic folklore—from a cursed princess born under an eclipse and a lovelorn beast in a forest mansion to the fateful banquet in Cintra that binds Geralt\'s destiny through the \"Law of Surprise\" to an unborn child.'),(61,'The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe','C.S. Lewis','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'narnia, wardrobe, aslan, magic, winter','Sent away from London during the World War II Blitz to stay at the sprawling country house of an eccentric old professor, the four Pevensie siblings—Peter, Susan, Edmund, and Lucy—pass a rainy afternoon playing hide-and-seek. Stepping inside a large wooden wardrobe in a spare room, youngest sister Lucy pushes past the fur coats only to find herself standing in a snowy forest under the light of a lamppost.\n\nSoon all four children enter the magical land of Narnia, which has been cursed by the tyrannical White Witch so that it is \"always winter, and never Christmas.\" When Edmund is lured into betraying his siblings by the Witch\'s enchanted Turkish Delight, the children must join forces with the great lion Aslan to fulfill an ancient prophecy and break the Witch\'s reign.'),(62,'A Court of Thorns and Roses','Sarah J. Maas','Fantasy',2,0,'no',0,'fae, magic, curse, high fantasy, romance','In a cold, impoverished cottage near the border wall separating the mortal realm from the magical lands of Prythian, nineteen-year-old Feyre Archeron is the sole provider for her crippled father and two demanding sisters. When she ventures deep into the winter woods and shoots a massive wolf with an ash arrow to keep her family from starving, a terrifying beast bursts into her home demanding retribution.\n\nBound by an ancient treaty, Feyre is taken across the wall to the Spring Court, where she discovers her captor is not an animal at all, but Tamlin—one of the lethal, immortal High Fae who once ruled the world. As Feyre\'s hostility toward Tamlin softens into a passionate connection, a shadowy blight spreads across the faerie lands, forcing her to risk everything to break a deadly curse.'),(63,'It Ends with Us','Colleen Hoover','Romance',2,0,'no',0,'contemporary, relationships, drama, love, emotional','Having grown up in a small town in Maine watching her abusive father mistreat her mother, Lily Bloom has worked hard to build an independent new life in Boston and open her own flower shop. On the night of her father\'s funeral, she meets Ryle Kincaid on a city rooftop—an assertive, brilliant neurosurgeon who has a strict rule against dating, yet finds himself irresistibly drawn to Lily.\n\nAs Ryle\'s rules give way to a deeply romantic marriage, Lily\'s happiness is complicated both by the sudden reappearance of Atlas Corrigan—the homeless boy who was her first love and protector in high school—and by a terrifying moment of anger from Ryle that shatters her sense of safety. Lily is forced to confront the painful reality of generational trauma and what it truly takes to break an abusive cycle.'),(64,'The Seven Husbands of Evelyn Hugo','Taylor Jenkins Reid','Romance',2,0,'no',0,'hollywood, fame, secrets, glamorous, biography','Aging and reclusive Hollywood movie icon Evelyn Hugo is finally ready to tell the truth about her glamorous and scandalous life. When she chooses unknown magazine reporter Monique Grant to write her authorized biography, no one is more astounded than Monique herself, who is navigating a stalled career and an impending divorce.\n\nSummoned to Evelyn\'s luxurious Upper East Side apartment, Monique listens as the actress recounts her rise from Hell\'s Kitchen poverty in the 1950s to the height of the studio system, her ruthless ambition, her seven calculated marriages, and the one forbidden, lifelong love she spent decades protecting from the tabloids. As the narrative nears its end, Monique discovers how Evelyn\'s history is tragically intertwined with her own.'),(65,'Normal People','Sally Rooney','Romance',2,0,'no',0,'coming of age, modern romance, connection, youth','In a small town in the west of Ireland, Connell and Marianne pretend not to know each other in the hallways of their secondary school. He is a popular, well-liked star of the football team, while she is a proud, solitary outcast from a wealthy, dysfunctional family—and Connell\'s mother works as a cleaner in Marianne\'s mansion. When an awkward conversation in Marianne\'s kitchen sparks an intense, secret romance, it sets in motion a bond neither of them can ever fully sever.\n\nA year later, both arrive at Trinity College in Dublin, where their social roles invert: Marianne blossoms among sophisticated peers while Connell struggles with class anxiety and shyness. Across four years of coming together and drifting apart, Sally Rooney traces the complexities of intimacy, social class, mental health, and how two people can profoundly reshape each other\'s lives.'),(66,'Outlander','Diana Gabaldon','Romance',2,0,'no',0,'time travel, scotland, historical romance, highlands','In 1945, former British combat nurse Claire Randall travels to the Scottish Highlands with her husband Frank for a second honeymoon, hoping to reconnect after years of separation during World War II. While visiting an ancient circle of standing stones at Craigh na Dun, Claire touches a cleft rock and is hurtled two hundred years into the past, waking up in a Scotland torn apart by clan warfare and British Redcoat patrols in the year 1743.\n\nTaken in by Clan MacKenzie at Castle Leoch and forced by circumstance to marry Jamie Fraser, a gallant and fugitive young Highland warrior, Claire uses her twentieth-century medical knowledge and historical foresight to survive. Soon, her fierce loyalty and passion for Jamie leave her impossibly torn between two men in two irreconcilable centuries.'),(67,'Me Before You','Jojo Moyes','Romance',2,0,'no',0,'drama, romance, emotional, tragedy, tearjerker','Louisa Clark is an ordinary twenty-six-year-old living in a small English castle town: she loves quirky outfits, lives with her noisy working-class family, and has barely stepped outside her comfort zone. When the local café where she works abruptly closes, Lou desperately accepts a six-month job as a companion to Will Traynor, a wealthy former London financier and extreme-sports enthusiast who was left quadriplegic after a motorcycle accident two years earlier.\n\nEmbittered by the loss of his active life and in constant physical pain, Will is initially cold and sardonic toward his cheerful new caregiver. When Lou overhears a devastating secret about why she was hired for precisely six months, she sets out to organize a series of adventures to convince Will that his life is still worth living.'),(68,'The Notebook','Nicholas Sparks','Romance',2,0,'no',0,'classic romance, southern, devotion, timeless','In a quiet nursing home, an elderly man opens a faded notebook and reads aloud to a woman suffering from Alzheimer\'s disease, hoping the familiar words will trigger a miracle. The story inside takes us back to the summer of 1932 in New Bern, North Carolina, where seventeen-year-old Allie Nelson, the daughter of a wealthy aristocratic family, falls deeply in love with Noah Calhoun, a poetic local boy who works at a lumberyard.\n\nSeparated at summer\'s end by Allie\'s disapproving mother, who intercepts Noah\'s letters for years, the two move on with their lives until 1946, when Allie—now engaged to a prominent lawyer—sees a newspaper photo of Noah standing in front of the restored plantation house he promised her as a teenager. Returning to New Bern for one final visit, she must choose between social expectation and an enduring love.'),(69,'Wuthering Heights','Emily Brontë','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'gothic, passion, obsession, yorkshire, classic','Arriving as the new tenant at Thrushcross Grange on the desolate Yorkshire moors, Mr. Lockwood visits his sour, tyrannical landlord, Heathcliff, at the neighboring farmhouse of Wuthering Heights. Snowed in overnight and shaken by a terrifying spectral encounter at his window, Lockwood asks his housekeeper, Nelly Dean, to recount the history of the two estates.\n\nNelly unfolds a dark, generational saga beginning thirty years earlier, when Mr. Earnshaw brought home a ragged, dark-skinned foundling named Heathcliff to be raised alongside his children, Hindley and Catherine. While Hindley brutally abuses the boy, Catherine and Heathcliff forge a fierce, untamed bond—until Catherine chooses social status by marrying the refined Edgar Linton, unleashing Heathcliff\'s lifelong campaign of obsessive vengeance against both families.'),(70,'Emma','Jane Austen','Literature',2,0,'no',0,'regency, matchmaking, wit, high society, austen','Handsome, clever, and rich, twenty-year-old Emma Woodhouse reigns as the undisputed queen of Highbury society and sees no need to ever marry. Having just taken credit for arranging the happy marriage of her beloved former governess, Emma decides to make matchmaking her favorite pastime—ignoring the sensible warnings of her sharp-eyed brother-in-law and confidant, Mr. George Knightley.\n\nTaking the sweet, unassuming parlor-boarder Harriet Smith under her wing, Emma persuades Harriet to reject a prosperous young farmer\'s proposal in favor of the ambitious local vicar, Mr. Elton. When Emma\'s misreadings of her neighbors\' hearts repeatedly backfire, she is forced to confront her own vanity and realize where her true affections have belonged all along.'),(71,'The Shining','Stephen King','Horror',2,0,'no',0,'hotel, winter, psychological horror, isolation','Hoping for a fresh start after losing his teaching job due to his explosive temper and alcoholism, aspiring writer Jack Torrance accepts the job of winter caretaker at the historic Overlook Hotel high in the Colorado Rockies. Moving into the sprawling, empty resort with his wife Wendy and their five-year-old son Danny, Jack plans to use the five months of snowbound isolation to sober up and finish his play.\n\nDanny, however, possesses \"the shining\"—a powerful psychic gift that allows him to read minds and glimpse the hotel\'s gruesome history of murder and suicide. As winter blizzards cut the family off from the outside world, the malevolent supernatural forces inhabiting the Overlook feed on Danny\'s gift and slowly drive Jack toward homicidal madness.'),(72,'Dracula','Bram Stoker','Horror',2,0,'no',0,'vampire, gothic horror, victorian, epistolary','Told entirely through diary entries, letters, ship\'s logs, and newspaper clippings, Bram Stoker\'s gothic classic begins when young English solicitor Jonathan Harker travels to a crumbling castle in the Carpathian Mountains of Transylvania to finalize a real estate purchase in London for an aristocratic client named Count Dracula. Before long, Harker realizes he is a prisoner in the castle and that his nocturnal host is an ancient, undead predator.\n\nEscaping to England aboard a Russian schooner whose crew is mysteriously slaughtered en route, Dracula sets his sights on Harker\'s fiancée, Mina Murray, and her high-spirited friend Lucy Westenra. To stop the Count from spreading his vampiric curse across Victorian London, Harker bands together with Dutch polymath Professor Abraham Van Helsing and Lucy\'s suitors in a desperate hunt from London back to Transylvania.'),(73,'A Study in Scarlet','Arthur Conan Doyle','Mystery',2,0,'no',0,'sherlock holmes, detective, london, watson','Returned to London after being wounded in the Second Anglo-Afghan War, army surgeon Dr. John Watson is searching for affordable lodgings when an acquaintance introduces him to Sherlock Holmes, an eccentric consulting detective who spends his days conducting chemical experiments and studying crime. Moving into 221B Baker Street together, Watson is immediately fascinated by his flatmate\'s uncanny powers of observation and deduction.\n\nSoon, Scotland Yard inspectors Gregson and Lestrade call upon Holmes to examine a baffling crime inside an abandoned house in Lauriston Gardens: an American man lies dead with no wounds on his body, a woman\'s wedding ring on the floor, and the word \"RACHE\" scrawled in blood on the wall. The investigation uncovers a gripping tale of frontier vengeance stretching from Victorian London back to the Mormon settlements of Utah.'),(74,'All the Light We Cannot See','Anthony Doerr','Historical Fiction',2,0,'no',0,'wwii, france, radio, pulitzer, historical','Marie-Laure LeBlanc grows up blind in Paris near the Museum of Natural History, where her devoted father works as the master of its thousands of locks and builds miniature wooden models of their neighborhood so she can navigate the streets by touch. When the Nazis occupy Paris in 1940, father and daughter flee to the walled coastal citadel of Saint-Malo to live with a shell-shocked great-uncle, unknowingly carrying what may be the museum\'s most valuable and cursed diamond.\n\nHundreds of miles away in a German mining town, orphan Werner Pfennig\'s extraordinary gift for building and fixing shortwave radios earns him a place at a brutal academy for Hitler Youth and eventually assigns him to a special unit tracking resistance broadcasts across Europe. As Allied bombs fall on Saint-Malo in 1944, Marie-Laure\'s and Werner\'s paths converge in an unforgettable story of resilience and human connection.'),(75,'Where the Crawdads Sing','Delia Owens','Mystery',2,0,'no',0,'marsh, murder mystery, nature, survival, drama','For years, rumors of the \"Marsh Girl\" have haunted Barkley Cove, a quiet fishing village on the coast of North Carolina. Abandoned one by one by her mother, siblings, and abusive father before she reaches age ten, Kya Clark survives alone in the lush coastal marshes—trading freshly dug mussels for gas and grits, learning to read from a kindly friend named Tate Walker, and finding solace among the gulls and tidal channels.\n\nDrawn out of her isolation as a young woman by the yearning to be touched and loved, Kya enters a secret involvement with Chase Andrews, the town\'s handsome former quarterback. When Chase\'s body is found beneath a dilapidated fire tower in late 1969, the townspeople immediately turn their suspicions on the reclusive naturalist, setting the stage for a gripping murder trial.'),(76,'The Song of Achilles','Madeline Miller','Romance',2,0,'no',0,'mythology, trojan war, greece, achilles, epic','Exiled as a boy after an accidental killing, the awkward, unremarkable prince Patroclus is taken into the court of King Peleus of Phthia. There, despite being shunned by the other boys, he is chosen as the closest companion of Peleus\'s son Achilles—golden, swift, and destined by prophecy to become the greatest warrior Greece has ever known.\n\nAs the two grow into young men and train under the wise centaur Chiron on Mount Pelion, their bond deepens into a luminous, all-consuming love, much to the displeasure of Achilles\'s mother, the cruel sea goddess Thetis. When word arrives that Helen of Sparta has been abducted, the heroes of Greece are summoned to lay siege to Troy, forcing Achilles and Patroclus into a ten-year war where fate, honor, and pride threaten to tear them apart.');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `books` ENABLE KEYS */;
